INT. FLOODED SIGNAL BUNKER - NIGHT

A concrete cell below a ruined rail yard. Knee-deep black water. Shells land overhead in a steady rhythm; dust sifts from the ceiling with each one.

SERGEANT IMRE VÁRHEGYI (30s, radio operator, face smeared with soot, headset over a bandaged ear) crouches at a field set balanced on a stack of ammunition crates. He holds the handset in both fists.

IMRE
(into handset)
Wolf Three, this is Hearth. Say again your last.

Static. A shell lands. A pipe bursts somewhere and a thin stream of water begins to fall on the radio. He shields it with his body.

COMMANDER (V.O.)
(through the handset, thin)
Hearth, Wolf Three. Grid is compromised. Repeat, grid is compromised. Fall back to the--

The voice breaks into crackling.

IMRE
Wolf Three, you are cutting out. Say again fall back to where?

Static. Another impact, closer. A chunk of ceiling drops into the water beside him. He does not flinch; he wipes the handset on his sleeve.

IMRE (CONT'D)
(into handset)
Wolf Three, Hearth. Do you copy? Wolf Three.

COMMANDER (V.O.)
(faint)
--the old chapel. East of the water tower. Bring the set if you can carry it.

IMRE
The set weighs forty kilos, sir.

COMMANDER (V.O.)
Then leave it, Sergeant. Leave it and run.

He looks at the radio. At the water rising around his thighs. At the iron ladder bolted to the wall, three rungs of it underwater.

IMRE
(into handset)
Sir, if I leave it, the battalion has no voice.

COMMANDER (V.O.)
The battalion has no battalion, Imre. Go.

A long beat. He sets the handset down on the crate, carefully, like something sleeping. Reaches under the set and unclips the battery. Pulls the crystal. Pockets both.

IMRE
(muttered)
Tell it to the next man.

He wades to the ladder. Behind him the handset crackles to itself.

COMMANDER (V.O.)
(faint, alone)
Hearth? Hearth, are you there?

Imre stops on the ladder with one boot on the lowest dry rung. He climbs back down. Picks up the handset.

IMRE
(into handset)
Wolf Three, Hearth. I am here.

A shell lands. The lamp dies. Darkness, and the sound of rushing water.
